boot.img is the core file that starts up Android, while vbmeta.img is the security file that checks if your system has been tampered with. Both files are critical to how your phone powers on and verifies its software.

1. What boot.img Is Used For

boot.img contains the essential components needed to wake up your phone's hardware and load the Android operating system.
• Contains the Linux Kernel: It holds the core software that talks directly to your phone's processor, screen, battery, and memory.
• Holds the Ramdisk: It includes tiny startup files that prepare the file system before the main Android interface loads.
• Used for Rooting: Tools like Magisk or KernelSU modify boot.img (or init_boot.img on newer phones) to give you administrator (root) access when the phone starts up.
• Used for Fixing Bootloops: Flashing a clean, original boot.img fixes a phone that gets stuck on the startup logo after a failed modification.

2. What vbmeta.img Is Used For

vbmeta.img (Verified Boot Metadata) acts as a digital security guard for Android Verified Boot (AVB).
• Checks System Integrity: It stores cryptographic keys and hashes used to verify that partitions like boot, system, and vendor have not been altered by viruses or unauthorized modifications.
• Blocks Modified Software: If you flash a custom recovery (like TWRP) or a custom ROM, vbmeta.img will detect the change and block the phone from booting unless verification is turned off.
• Used to Disable Security Checks: Modders flash a blank or patched vbmeta.img using special fastboot flags (--disable-verity --disable-verification) so the phone allows custom software to run.

Summary Comparison


Feature	boot.img	vbmeta.img
Main Role	Boots the Android OS and controls hardware.	Verifies that OS partitions are original and safe.
What It Contains	Linux kernel and initial ramdisk.	Digital signatures, keys, and partition hashes.
Common Modding Use	Patched with Magisk to gain root access.	Flashed with disabled verification to allow custom ROMs/recoveries.

